.TH SPIMULATOR 1
.SH NAME
spimulator \- a MIPS32 simulator for teaching
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B spimulator
[\fIoptions\fP] [\fB\-file\fP \fIfile\fP ...] [\fIargs\fP ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBspimulator\fP assembles and runs MIPS32 assembly programs.  It is a fork of
James Larus's SPIM, maintained as a teaching tool: it adds a teaching mode that
narrates each instruction as it runs, and views of what the assembler builds.
.PP
With one or more \fB\-file\fP options (or a bare file name), it loads the
files, runs the program from \fB__start\fP, and exits with the program's
status.  With no file, it starts an interactive prompt that loads, runs,
steps and inspects programs; type \fBhelp\fP there for its commands.
.PP
The same engine is also implemented in Python, Haskell and Rust, as the
commands \fBspimulator\-python\fP, \fBspimulator\-haskell\fP and
\fBspimulator\-rust\fP.  They take the options below, except \fB\-explain\fP,
and give the same output and exit status.
.SH OPTIONS
Each option's short form follows it.  An option that takes a value must not be
the last argument.
.SS "The machine"
.IP "\fB\-asm\fP, \fB\-a\fP" 8
Simulate the virtual machine the assembler provides: pseudo-instructions,
no delayed branches or loads.  This is the default.
.IP "\fB\-bare\fP, \fB\-b\fP" 8
Simulate a bare MIPS machine: no pseudo-instructions or extra addressing
modes, delayed branches and loads.  Implies \fB\-quiet\fP.
.IP "\fB\-delayed_branches\fP, \fB\-db\fP" 8
Execute the instruction after a branch or jump (the delay slot) before
transferring control.
.IP "\fB\-delayed_loads\fP, \fB\-dl\fP" 8
Simulate non-interlocked loads: a loaded value is visible one instruction
later.
.IP "\fB\-pseudo\fP, \fB\-p\fP / \fB\-nopseudo\fP, \fB\-np\fP" 8
Accept (the default) or reject pseudo-instructions.
.SS "The exception handler"
.IP "\fB\-exception\fP, \fB\-e\fP / \fB\-trap\fP, \fB\-t\fP" 8
Load the exception handler and the startup code that calls \fBmain\fP.  This
is the default; the handler built into the program is used.
.IP "\fB\-noexception\fP, \fB\-ne\fP / \fB\-notrap\fP, \fB\-nt\fP" 8
Load neither.  Execution then starts at the label \fB__start\fP, and an
exception jumps to 0x80000180, which must hold a handler.
.IP "\fB\-exception_file\fP \fIfile\fP, \fB\-ef\fP / \fB\-trap_file\fP \fIfile\fP, \fB\-tf\fP" 8
Load the exception handler and startup code from \fIfile\fP instead of the
built-in one.
.SS Messages
.IP "\fB\-quiet\fP, \fB\-q\fP" 8
Do not print warnings, such as the message at an exception.
.IP "\fB\-noquiet\fP, \fB\-nq\fP" 8
Print warnings.  This is the default.
.SS "Teaching mode"
.IP "\fB-explain\fP [\fIN\fP]" 10
Enable teaching mode. \fIspim\fP first prints a listing of the loaded
program and a one-time note on the output format; then, after each
instruction executes, it narrates the step on standard error. The
program runs exactly as it does without \fB-explain\fP: its output, its
exceptions and its exit status are the same. The optional level \fIN\fP
selects how much detail is shown:
.RS
.IP "\fBLevel 1\fP \(em step header" 14
The PC, the instruction's encoding, its disassembly and its source line.
An instruction the assembler emitted for a pseudo-op (or for an
instruction it had to expand, such as \fBlw\fP from a label) gets a
one-line note naming it and its place in the expansion. When the
program exits, the exit status is shown.
.IP "\fBLevel 2\fP \(em + narration" 14
Adds, per instruction: a description of the pseudo-op it came from, its
category and mnemonic modifiers, what it did in plain English, the
\fIInputs\fP it read and what it \fIWrote\fP (registers, and memory for
stores), \fITry it yourself\fP hints showing the \fIprint\fP commands that
inspect the relevant state, syscall details, and the PC change.
.IP "\fBLevel 3\fP \(em + bit-layout" 14
Adds the 32-bit instruction's bit-field diagram (opcode / rs / rt / rd /
shamt / funct for R-type, opcode / rs / rt / immediate for I-type, opcode /
target for J-type) and which bit pattern selected this instruction.
.IP "\fBLevel 4\fP \(em + decoder walkthrough" 14
Replaces the L3 diagram with a step-by-step decoder walkthrough: the same
box is redrawn 2\(en3 times, with one more field filled in each frame,
mirroring the CPU's hierarchical decoding (opcode \(-> sub-table selector
\(-> remaining fields). The final frame is identical to the L3 box.
.RE
.IP "" 10
With no argument, \fB-explain\fP defaults to level 3 (the historical full
output). The level may also be given with an equals sign: \fB-explain=2\fP.
Short form: \fB-x\fP (\fB-x 1\fP, \fB-x=2\fP, etc.); \fB-explain=0\fP turns it off.
The ports (below) do not have the teaching mode yet, and reject this option.
.IP "\fB\-noexplain\fP, \fB\-nx\fP" 8
Turn the teaching mode off.  This is the default.
.SS "Memory-mapped IO"
.IP "\fB\-mapped_io\fP, \fB\-mio\fP" 8
Enable the memory-mapped terminal device.  A program that reads the terminal
through syscalls cannot also use it.
.IP "\fB\-nomapped_io\fP, \fB\-nmio\fP" 8
Disable it.  This is the default.
.SS "Memory sizes"
.IP "\fB\-stext\fP, \fB\-st\fP; \fB\-sdata\fP, \fB\-sd\fP; \fB\-sstack\fP, \fB\-ss\fP; \fB\-sktext\fP, \fB\-skt\fP; \fB\-skdata\fP, \fB\-skd\fP \fIsize\fP" 8
The initial size, in bytes, of the text, data, stack, kernel text and kernel
data segments.  The kernel segments hold the exception handler; there is no
kernel stack.
.IP "\fB\-ldata\fP, \fB\-ld\fP; \fB\-lstack\fP, \fB\-ls\fP; \fB\-lkdata\fP, \fB\-lkd\fP \fIsize\fP" 8
The limit, in bytes, that the data, stack and kernel data segments may grow
to.
.SS "Loading and running"
.IP "\fB\-file\fP \fIfile\fP, \fB\-f\fP \fIfile\fP" 8
Load \fIfile\fP.  The option may be repeated: the files' text, data and
symbols accumulate into one symbol table, so references resolve across
files, as with the prompt's \fBload\fP command; there is no separate link
step.  A bare argument that is not an option is loaded as a file too.  The
arguments after the last file are the program's arguments; its argv[0] is the
last file loaded.
.SS "What the assembler builds"
Each of these assembles the files and, instead of running the program, or
before it, writes:
.IP "\fB\-assemble\fP" 8
the assembled code, to \fIfile\fP.out.
.IP "\fB\-dump\fP" 8
the user text and data segments, to text.asm and data.asm.
.IP "\fB\-full_dump\fP" 8
the user and kernel text and data segments, to text.asm and data.asm.
.IP "\fB\-listing\fP \fIfile\fP" 8
an assemble-time event trace (labels defined, instructions and data emitted,
forward references resolved, each with its source line) to \fIfile\fP, or to
standard error for \fB\-\fP.  The program then runs.
.IP "\fB\-print\-ast\fP" 8
the parsed program as an indented tree, to standard error; then exit.
.IP "\fB\-print\-ast\-json\fP" 8
the same tree as JSON, to standard output; then exit.
.IP "\fB\-show\-expansion\fP" 8
just the pseudo-instructions and what each becomes, to standard error; then
exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.IP \(bu 3
The program's status: the value it passes to the exit2 syscall (17), or
\fBmain\fP's return value, which the startup code passes on.
.IP \(bu 3
128 plus the exception code, if an exception occurred and the program did not
set a non-zero status itself (an unaligned load exits 132).
.IP \(bu 3
2 after an assembly error, 1 when the files cannot be loaded or a symbol is
undefined, and 255 after an internal error.
.SH FILES
.IP src/exceptions.s 8
The source of the default exception handler, built into the program.
